    "proved": "A nonnegative auxiliary curve coefficient preserves the separated product locus, both normalized model projections, its complete geodesic polar metric, analytic ideal closure and unique nearest-point ideal retraction. Its diameter is exactly twice the sum of the curve areas, including at the nonbig zero-coefficient background.",
    "mechanism": "Restrictions to base sections compute the ordinary projection. Atomic Green potentials and countable-divisor domination compute the ideal projection without assuming strict positivity of the base. Unit-divisor fibration reduction gives the upstairs identities. The fixed functions transfer the metric, ideal projection and analytic closure through the nonclosed coefficient. Extremal atomic measures give the diameter; a closed positive test form and Stokes prove that the zero background is not big.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only the specified separated curve product locus is covered. No unrestricted higher-dimensional metric or capacity assertion upstairs is claimed. The top-volume comparison expression vanishes, including at the nonbig limit; no big-background theorem is applied there."
